      "the_aversives_in_the_history": "The aversives in this history",
      "the_aversives_in_the_history_desc": "The timeline in this tool runs through the early intensive-intervention programs without saying what was in them. It should. Lovaas’s UCLA work in the 1960s used contingent electric shock, and he published it; the 1987 study’s treatment package included aversives such as a loud “no” and a slap on the thigh. Mainstream practice moved away from aversives and the BACB now restricts them sharply — but contingent shock has not ended everywhere, and its legal status in the United States has changed more than once. When autistic adults describe early ABA as harmful, this is a large part of what they are describing. A tool that teaches positive punishment as a quadrant on a chart owes students the version where it was a real thing done to real children.",
